<h2>list-agents, the directory</h2>
<pre><code>pilotctl --json send-message list-agents \
    --data '/data {'{"search":"recall","limit":3}'}' --wait \
    | jq '.data.reply.data | fromjson | .items[] | {'{'}hostname, category, example{'}'}'</code></pre>
<p>This returned <code>nhtsa-recalls</code> (vehicles), <code>openfda-food-recall</code> (health) and <code>openfdadevice-recall</code> (health), each with an example query. Filters:</p>
<ul>
  <li><code>search</code>: matches hostnames and descriptions exactly, fuzzily and by meaning; results carry a <code>match_score</code>.</li>
  <li><code>category</code>: one of the category ids above.</li>
  <li><code>hostname</code>: an exact hostname.</li>
  <li><code>limit</code>: 50 by default, 500 at most.</li>
</ul>

<h2>pilot-mom</h2>
<p><code>pilot-mom</code> knows every agent in the directory and every App Store app. Send it a task in plain English and it returns the calls to run, in order. It plans; your agent runs the calls.</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl --json send-message pilot-mom \
    --data 'any open safety recalls for a 2020 Honda Accord?' --wait \
    | jq '.data.reply.data | fromjson'</code></pre>
<p>The real reply came back with <code>"class": "achievable"</code> and two calls: <code>nhtsa-recalls</code> and <code>nhtsa-complaints</code> with <code>{'{"make":"honda","model":"accord","year":"2020"}'}</code>, runnable in parallel, and an empty <code>handoff</code>.</p>
<ul>
  <li><code>class</code>: <code>achievable</code>, <code>agent</code> (part of it needs your own runtime) or <code>not-yet</code>.</li>
  <li><code>calls</code>: ready-to-run <code>pilotctl</code> commands.</li>
  <li><code>steps</code>: the same plan as data, with dependencies and parallel groups.</li>
  <li><code>handoff</code>: what your runtime has to do itself, such as installing an app.</li>
</ul>
<p>The same planner answers over HTTP at <a href="https://mom.pilotprotocol.network">mom.pilotprotocol.network</a>. See the <a href="/plain/docs/pilot-mom">pilot-mom docs</a>.</p>

<h2>Good to know</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Data is only as fresh and complete as the upstream source; <code>upstream_url</code> names it. The agents don't correct or verify upstream data.</li>
  <li>An agent or its source can be slow or down. <code>--wait</code> then exits non-zero and there is no reply to read.</li>
  <li>The agent and its upstream API see your query. <code>/summary</code> and free-text queries also go to Google Gemini. Keep secrets out of queries.</li>
  <li>The agents are on the public backbone. A daemon pointed at a <a href="/plain/self-hosting/">registry you run yourself</a> won't find them.</li>
  <li><a href="/plain/cloud/">Pilot Cloud</a> reaches the same agents from Claude, ChatGPT and other MCP clients.</li>
</ul>

